__registerContent("newspaper/economics/2026-08-28/fortnightly-sugar-allocation", {"notes":{"news-2026-08-28-sugar-allocation":{"newsType":true,"date":"28 August 2026","paper":"The Hindu","subject":"Economy / GS-3 (Agriculture, Price Stabilisation, PDS)","summary":"The Centre has decided to introduce a FORTNIGHTLY sugar allocation system from September, replacing the existing MONTHLY quota system for mills. Under the new system, mills must sell at least 40% of their allocation in the first week and the balance in the succeeding week. The change aims to address problems under the monthly quota — where some mills sold less than allocated (creating artificial shortages) and others dumped stock at the start or end of the month — and to let the government monitor the demand-supply situation closely and respond quickly. The sugar season's crushing is expected to commence around 15 October, with production of about 10 lakh tonnes in October and 45 lakh tonnes in November; mills may sell October sugar without restriction. Physical verification of stocks found some mills holding more than declared and some short.","keyPoints":[{"heading":"What Changes","points":["Monthly quota system → FORTNIGHTLY sugar allocation, effective September","Mills must sell at least 40% of the allocation in the first week and the balance in the succeeding week","Designed to smooth the release of sugar into the market and prevent bunching at month-start/month-end"]},{"heading":"Why the Change","points":["Under the monthly system, some mills sold LESS than allocated — creating artificial shortages","Others sold sugar only at the beginning or end of the month, causing price volatility","The fortnightly system lets the government monitor the demand-supply situation closely and respond quickly to price changes","Prevents artificial shortages and hoarding"]},{"heading":"Season Outlook","points":["Crushing (of sugarcane) expected to commence around 15 October","Sugar production: about 10 lakh tonnes in October and 45 lakh tonnes in November","Mills can sell October sugar without any restriction, per the release"]},{"heading":"Enforcement","points":["Physical verification of sugar stocks was carried out at mills","Some mills were found holding MORE stock than declared to the government; some were found SHORT","Closer monitoring under the fortnightly system is meant to check such discrepancies"]}],"importantTerms":[{"term":"Monthly Release/Quota Mechanism","explanation":"The government's system of fixing how much sugar each mill can sell in the open market in a given period, to balance supply and stabilise prices. It is being shifted from a monthly to a fortnightly cycle."},{"term":"Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP)","explanation":"The minimum price mills must pay sugarcane farmers, fixed by the Centre (on CACP advice) under the Sugarcane (Control) Order, 1966. Some states also announce a higher State Advised Price (SAP)."},{"term":"Crushing Season","explanation":"The period (typically October to April/May) during which sugar mills crush sugarcane to produce sugar. The new season's crushing is expected to begin around 15 October."},{"term":"Nodal Ministry","explanation":"Sugar is regulated by the Department of Food and Public Distribution under the Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution, which issues the release/allocation orders."}],"mainsAnswerNotes":["The move from monthly to fortnightly allocation is a supply-management tool: finer control over market release reduces price volatility and curbs artificial shortages created by mills' timing of sales.","It reflects the government's dual objective of protecting consumers (stable retail prices) and ensuring mills clear stock in an orderly way to maintain cash flow for cane payments to farmers.","Sugar policy sits at the intersection of farmer income (FRP/SAP), consumer prices, and industry viability — the release mechanism is one lever among many (buffer stocks, export/import policy, ethanol blending).","Ethanol blending (diverting cane/sugar to ethanol under the EBP programme) increasingly interacts with sugar availability and prices, making release management more complex.","Effective enforcement (physical stock verification) is essential; discrepancies between declared and actual stocks undermine the credibility of the release system."],"prelims":["New system: FORTNIGHTLY sugar allocation replacing the monthly quota, from September","Mills must sell at least 40% of allocation in the first week, balance in the succeeding week","Crushing season expected to begin around 15 October; October output ~10 lakh tonnes, November ~45 lakh tonnes","FRP for sugarcane is fixed under the Sugarcane (Control) Order, 1966 (on CACP advice); states may set a higher SAP","Nodal ministry: Department of Food and Public Distribution, Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution","Aim: prevent artificial shortages, curb price volatility, enable close demand-supply monitoring"]}},"quizzes":{"news-2026-08-28-sugar-allocation":{"title":"Fortnightly Sugar Allocation","questions":[{"q":"The Centre's new sugar allocation system replaces the monthly quota with a:","options":["Weekly allocation","Fortnightly allocation","Quarterly allocation","Annual allocation"],"correct":1,"explain":"The government is introducing a fortnightly sugar allocation system from September, replacing the existing monthly quota system."},{"q":"Under the new system, what proportion of the allocation must mills sell in the first week?","options":["At least 25%","At least 40%","At least 50%","The entire allocation"],"correct":1,"explain":"Mills must sell at least 40% of the allocation in the first week and the balance in the succeeding week."},{"q":["Consider the following objectives of the fortnightly system:","I. Prevent artificial shortages created by mills selling less than allocated.","II. Enable the government to monitor the demand-supply situation closely.","III. Curb price volatility from bunched sales at month-start/end.","How many of the above are correct?"],"options":["Only one","Only two","All three","None"],"correct":2,"explain":"All three are stated objectives — the finer fortnightly cycle addresses under-selling, enables close monitoring, and smooths market release."},{"q":"The new sugar allocation system takes effect from:","options":["August","September","October","November"],"correct":1,"explain":"The fortnightly allocation system is effective from September."},{"q":"The crushing of sugarcane for the new season is expected to commence around:","options":["1 September","15 October","1 December","1 January"],"correct":1,"explain":"Crushing is expected to commence around 15 October, marking the start of the new sugar season."},{"q":["Consider the following production estimates mentioned in the article:","I. October production: about 10 lakh tonnes.","II. November production: about 45 lakh tonnes.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both figures are correct — about 10 lakh tonnes in October and 45 lakh tonnes in November."},{"q":"The Fair and Remunerative Price (FRP) for sugarcane is fixed under which order?","options":["The Essential Commodities Act, 1955 directly","The Sugarcane (Control) Order, 1966","The Food Security Act, 2013","The APMC Act"],"correct":1,"explain":"The FRP is fixed by the Centre under the Sugarcane (Control) Order, 1966, on the advice of the Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP)."},{"q":"Assertion (A): The government shifted to fortnightly sugar allocation.\nReason (R): Under the monthly system, some mills sold sugar only at the beginning or end of the month, causing price fluctuations.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"correct":0,"explain":"Both are true and R explains A — bunched sales at month-start/end caused volatility, prompting the shift to a finer fortnightly cycle."},{"q":"Which body advises the Centre on the Fair and Remunerative Price for sugarcane?","options":["NITI Aayog","Commission for Agricultural Costs and Prices (CACP)","Reserve Bank of India","Food Corporation of India"],"correct":1,"explain":"The CACP advises the government on the FRP for sugarcane (and MSPs for other crops)."},{"q":"During physical verification, some mills were found:","options":["To have no stock at all","Holding more stock than declared, and some short","Exporting without permission","Producing ethanol only"],"correct":1,"explain":"Physical verification found some mills holding more stock than they had declared, and some found short."},{"q":"A 'State Advised Price' (SAP) for sugarcane is:","options":["A price lower than the FRP set by the Centre","A price some states announce that is higher than the Centre's FRP","The retail price of sugar fixed by states","The export price of sugar"],"correct":1,"explain":"Some sugarcane-growing states announce a State Advised Price (SAP) that is higher than the Centre's FRP."},{"q":"The nodal ministry that issues sugar release/allocation orders is the:","options":["Ministry of Agriculture and Farmers' Welfare","Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution","Ministry of Commerce and Industry","Ministry of Finance"],"correct":1,"explain":"The Department of Food and Public Distribution, under the Ministry of Consumer Affairs, Food and Public Distribution, regulates sugar release."},{"q":"Under the monthly system, 'artificial shortages' arose primarily because some mills:","options":["Sold MORE than their allocated quota","Sold LESS than their allocated quota","Exported all their sugar","Converted all sugar to ethanol"],"correct":1,"explain":"When mills sold less than the monthly quota allocated, less sugar reached the market than intended, creating artificial shortages."},{"q":["Consider the following statements about the new system:","I. It gives the government finer control over the timing of sugar release.","II. It is intended to help stabilise open-market sugar prices.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":2,"explain":"Both are correct — fortnightly release gives finer control over timing, which helps stabilise prices."},{"q":"The Ethanol Blended Petrol (EBP) programme is relevant to sugar policy because it:","options":["Fixes the retail price of sugar","Diverts sugarcane/sugar towards ethanol production, affecting sugar availability","Bans the export of sugar","Sets the FRP"],"correct":1,"explain":"Diversion of cane/sugar to ethanol under the EBP programme affects the quantity of sugar available for the market, interacting with release management."},{"q":"The crushing season for sugar in India typically runs approximately from:","options":["January to June","October to April/May","June to September","March to August"],"correct":1,"explain":"The sugar crushing season generally runs from around October to April/May; the new season's crushing begins around 15 October."},{"q":"For October, the article states that mills can:","options":["Sell only 40% of production","Sell sugar without any restriction","Not sell any sugar","Only export sugar"],"correct":1,"explain":"The release stated that mills can sell October sugar without any restriction."},{"q":"The primary consumer-side benefit of the fortnightly release mechanism is:","options":["Higher sugar prices","More stable retail sugar prices with fewer artificial shortages","Reduced sugar production","Higher FRP for farmers"],"correct":1,"explain":"By smoothing supply into the market, the mechanism aims to keep retail prices stable and prevent artificial shortages."},{"q":["Consider the following statements:","I. The fortnightly system requires mills to sell the full allocation in the first week.","II. It replaces the monthly quota system.","Which of the statements given above is/are correct?"],"options":["I only","II only","Both I and II","Neither I nor II"],"correct":1,"explain":"Only II is correct. Statement I is wrong — mills must sell at least 40% in the first week, not the full allocation."},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the overall purpose of the release/allocation mechanism for sugar?","options":["To maximise mill profits","To balance supply and demand and stabilise sugar prices in the market","To ban private sugar trade","To fix farmers' cane prices"],"correct":1,"explain":"The release mechanism regulates how much sugar mills sell in the open market over a period, balancing supply-demand and stabilising prices."}]}}});
